  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe main concern this forecast period will revolve around the \npotential for several rounds of rain today through Thursday night \npossibly leading to flooding. Today, an upper level ridge that has \nbuilt over the region will flatten out as a upper level shortwave \nmoves through the northern Plains into the Upper Midwest. At the \nsurface, a warm front will lift northward from the Red River Valley \ninto southeastern Kansas. While the surface feature will remain \nsouth of the area a weak LLJ will over run the front and nose into \nthe area today bringing the chance (30-50%) of showers and isolated \nthunderstorms to the western CWA. Highs today will range from the \nupper 70s to mid 80s. Tonight, rain changes will increase across the \nforecast area as the LLJ intensifies and becomes more focused over \nthe area. Best chances will be across the northwestern CWA where \nPoPs will be in the 70-90+% range whereas precipitation  chances \nsouth of Highway 50 are less than 15%. PWATs across the northern CWA \nwill range between 1.80\"-2.20\" which is around the 100th percentile \nfor early October. As such, heavy rain will possible, and \nconsequently a flood watch will go into effect after midnight across \nnorthwest Missouri and extreme northeastern Kansas. \n\nWednesday into Thursday, a closed upper level trough over the desert \nsouthwest will slowly move out into the Plains. Several lead \nshortwave will eject out of the feature bringing the chance for \ncontinued round of storms into the area. A persistent LLJ nosing \ninto the area will continue to provided added lift and moisture as \nPWAT will remain between 1.80\"-2.20\" during this period. Late \nThursday into Thursday night the closed upper trough over the \ncentral Plains will become an open wave and finally becomes more \nprogressive. The upper level trough will move through the area \nThursday night pushing a cold front through the area bringing one \nlast round of heavy rain to the area. All told, current forecast \nprecipitation amounts for this three day event range from 2-4+ \ninches for areas along and north of the Highway 36 corridor with 1-3 \ninches south. Antecedent conditions across northern Missouri after \nthis past weekend weather are wetter with 1-3\" having had fallen and \nwith the heavier precipitation expected to be focused there the \nflood watch that is out through Thursday night appears warranted. \nNot as much precipitation fell further south over the weekend and \nwith lesser amounts forecast we will hold off on expanding the watch \nalthough it may be necessary with further updates. Highs Wednesday \nwill be in the mid 70s to mid 80s with unsettled conditions keeping \nhighs in the upper 60s to upper 70s Thursday.\n\nConditions are expected to be mainly dry in the extended period \nalthough a few left over showers will be possible across the \nsouthern CWA on Friday. Much cooler conditions are expected Friday \nas well with highs in the mid 60s to lower 70s. Surface high \npressure then looks to build into the area for the weekend into \nearly next week. This will aid in keeping highs in the mid 60s to \nmid 70s.",
